-- allium: 3
--
-- Board — a puzzle in play
--
-- Scope
--   What stands between the rules of Sudoku and a player at a screen: one
--   puzzle as it is being played. The board keeps the puzzle, and through it
--   the givens, the player's digits, the conflicts and whether it is solved.
--   To that it adds what the rules leave out and a player expects: a note in
--   every cell, every move the player has made, so that the latest can be
--   taken back, one taken back can be re-taken, and the board as it stood
--   after any move can be read; one question about one cell, answered yes
--   or no; and a record, so that a board can be written down whole and had
--   again. It draws nothing. The rules stay sudoku.allium's, which this
--   module imports and never restates: in play every placement and erasure
--   enters through the Playing surface below, which puts it to the rules'
--   own PlaceDigit and EraseDigit, and sudoku.allium's PuzzleSolving stands
--   as the rules' own boundary, not a second way in.
--
-- Includes
--   - The board: one for every puzzle set, opened as the puzzle is and laid
--     out with one cell of its own for each cell of the grid. A record
--     reopened sets its puzzle again, so a reopened board is opened the
--     same way and then made whole from the record.
--   - Notes: each cell's marks, the digits the player has written in it to
--     say it might hold them. A mark goes only in an empty cell that is not
--     given. Writing a mark and striking one are the player's two moves on
--     a note, and moves in the full sense: recorded, taken back and re-taken
--     like a placement.
--   - Upkeep: when a digit is placed, that digit is struck from the note of
--     every peer, always, and which notes lost it is remembered so that undo
--     puts every struck mark back exactly. Erasing the digit gives nothing
--     back to the peers: only undo does, and only for the placement it takes
--     back, as on paper.
--   - The note beneath a digit: a placed cell keeps its note, unshown, and
--     shows it again when the digit is erased. Upkeep, undo and redo reach
--     it; nothing else does.
--   - Moves: every placement, erasure, written mark and struck mark, in the
--     order made, with what each displaced. Undo takes back the latest
--     standing move and leaves the board exactly as it stood before it; redo
--     re-takes the latest move undone; a new move discards every move undone.
--     Undo and redo stop when the puzzle is solved, because solved is final
--     in the rules.
--   - Reading back: the board as it stood once any move had been made and no
--     later one, standing or undone, solved or not, is read from the moves
--     and never by taking any back.
--   - The check: the player asks whether one cell's digit is the solution's
--     and hears yes or no, never a digit. Every check is kept, in order,
--     beside the moves; none is a move and none is taken back. Nothing else
--     in play consults the solution.
--   - The record: a board written down whole, and reopened from what was
--     written to the same board, moves, undone moves and checks included.
--     Recording says what that must satisfy and nothing of the form a record
--     takes or where one is kept.
--   - Two boundaries: Playing, which says what a play surface can see of a
--     board and what it may ask of it, and Reopening, where a board is
--     written down and had again and which stands whether or not a board
--     is open. Neither says how anything looks.
--
-- Excludes
--   - The rules: what a unit, a peer, a conflict and solved are, and what a
--     placement may do. sudoku.allium's, read through the puzzle and never
--     re-decided here. The guards a move restates below are the rules' own,
--     read from the puzzle so that a move the rules would refuse is never
--     recorded; one guard is the board's own, that a placement changes the
--     digit. Undo and redo write a cell's digit back to what a move the
--     rules admitted left there; they restore states the rules reached and
--     make no move of their own.
--   - Where a puzzle comes from. A board opens on whatever sudoku.allium's
--     PuzzleSetting admits, so this module has no setting surface of its own.
--     Reopening a record puts its givens to SetPuzzle, so a reopened board
--     too opens on what PuzzleSetting admits.
--   - Finding the solution. solution_digit_at below is a black box for that
--     reason, as solution_count is in sudoku.allium; solver.allium decides it
--     and is not imported.
--   - Hints, which are reach.allium's and stand in the context of the
--     board's puzzle; timers; and any count read off the checks, which is
--     the open question below.
--   - How a record is kept, and whether one is kept at all between visits.
--     The record is what a store is handed; the store is a port's.
--   - Anything drawn: how a note, a given, a conflict or a check's outcome
--     is shown, and the words a surface gives them. pawdoku.allium's Play
--     surface and the modules beneath it decide that, and the platform's
--     marks are theirs to translate.
--
-- Dependencies
--   sudoku.allium, for Puzzle, Cell, Given, the two moves and the side of
--   the grid. It does not import this module in return: a board reads its
--   puzzle and the puzzle knows nothing of the board.
--
-- Vocabulary
--   sudoku.allium's words stand: given, digit, unit, peer, conflict, solved.
--   technique.allium's mark stands, a candidate as the player has it
--   written, and human-solving.allium's note: one cell's marks. Never pencil
--   mark, never candidate for what the player writes. To them: board, the
--   puzzle in play with its notes, its moves and its checks. Move, one of
--   the four things a player does to a board; standing, of a move not taken
--   back; undone, of one that is. Undo and redo. Upkeep, the striking of a
--   placed digit from its peers' notes, as technique.allium has it, and
--   always whole here. Check, the player's question about one cell, which
--   in lapse.allium is a different thing under the same word. Record, a
--   board written down; reopen, to have it again.

use "./sudoku.allium" as sudoku

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The four things a player does to a board. A check is not among them: it
-- changes nothing and is never taken back.
enum MoveKind { place | erase | write_mark | strike_mark }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- External Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A board written down. What a record is made of is nobody's here: Recording
-- below says what writing one and reopening it must satisfy, and a store
-- behind a port decides the rest.
external entity Record {}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

entity Board {
    -- The puzzle in play. The givens, the digits, the conflicts and solved
    -- are read from it and never kept a second time.
    puzzle: sudoku/Puzzle

    cells: BoardCell with board = this
    moves: Move with board = this
    checks: Check with board = this

    standing_moves: moves where not is_undone
    undone_moves: moves where is_undone

    -- At most one each: the move undo takes back and the move redo re-takes.
    latest_standing_moves: moves where is_latest_standing
    next_to_redo: moves where is_next_to_redo

    -- Both stop with the puzzle: solved is final in the rules, and a board
    -- that could take a move back from a solved puzzle would un-solve it.
    can_undo: puzzle.status = unsolved and standing_moves.count > 0
    can_redo: puzzle.status = unsolved and undone_moves.count > 0
}

entity BoardCell {
    board: Board
    row: Integer
    column: Integer

    -- The puzzle's cell at this position: its digit, whether it is given,
    -- its peers and whether it conflicts are all read from here. One cell to
    -- a position in the rules, so this names one.
    puzzle_cell: sudoku/Cell
        with puzzle = this.board.puzzle and row = this.row and column = this.column

    -- The note: the marks the player has written in this cell. Kept while a
    -- digit stands in the cell and shown again when it is erased.
    note: Set<Integer>

    -- Erase and the check ask for a digit of the player's own.
    holds_players_digit: puzzle_cell.digit != null and not puzzle_cell.is_given

    -- A mark goes only where nothing stands and nothing was set.
    accepts_marks: puzzle_cell.digit = null and not puzzle_cell.is_given

    -- A note is shown while the cell is empty and waits beneath a digit.
    shows_note: puzzle_cell.digit = null
}

entity Move {
    board: Board

    -- One to move count, in the order made. An undone move keeps its index
    -- until a new move discards it.
    index: Integer
    kind: MoveKind
    target: BoardCell

    -- The digit placed, written or struck. Absent for an erasure.
    digit: Integer?

    -- What the target held before a placement or an erasure, so that undo
    -- can put it back. Absent for a move on a note.
    digit_before: Integer?

    -- The peers whose note lost the placed digit to upkeep, so that undo can
    -- give it back to those and no others. Empty for every other kind.
    struck_from: Set<BoardCell>

    is_undone: Boolean

    is_latest_standing:
        not is_undone and board.standing_moves.all(m => m.index <= this.index)
    is_next_to_redo:
        is_undone and board.undone_moves.all(m => m.index >= this.index)

    -- Reading back. digit_at_move and note_at_move are black boxes: the digit
    -- and the note cell c held once this move and no later one, standing or
    -- undone, had been made, read off the moves from the first to this one
    -- and never by taking any back. TheMovesReplayToTheBoard pins them to
    -- the board itself at the latest standing move.
    digit_after(c): digit_at_move(this, c)
    note_after(c): note_at_move(this, c)
}

-- One question asked and answered. Kept for as long as the board is, in the
-- order asked, beside the moves; never a move, never taken back.
entity Check {
    board: Board

    -- One to check count, in the order asked.
    index: Integer

    -- How many moves stood when the question was asked, so that a check can
    -- be read among the moves. A count and not a move: after an undo and a
    -- new move the same index names a different move, and the check still
    -- says how many stood.
    after_move: Integer

    target: BoardCell

    -- The digit the question was about, as it stood; the cell may since have
    -- changed.
    digit: Integer

    -- Whether that digit is the solution's. The solution itself is never a
    -- field of anything here.
    is_right: Boolean
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- sudoku.allium's figure under its name, as solver.allium restates it,
    -- so that no clause below carries a bare number.
    side: Integer = sudoku/config.side
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Opening ---------------------------------------------------

rule OpenBoard {
    when: puzzle: sudoku/Puzzle.created

    -- A board comes into being with its puzzle: whatever PuzzleSetting
    -- admits is a puzzle in play from the moment it is set.
    ensures: Board.created(puzzle: puzzle)
}

rule LayOutBoard {
    when: board: Board.created

    -- One cell of the board for each cell of the grid, as sudoku.allium lays
    -- the grid out. grid_positions is sudoku.allium's black box, read as it
    -- is there. Every note starts empty: nothing is written before the
    -- player writes it.
    ensures:
        for position in grid_positions(config.side):
            BoardCell.created(
                board: board,
                row: position.row,
                column: position.column,
                note: {}
            )
}

-- Moves -----------------------------------------------------

-- Every move discards whatever was undone and takes the next index, which
-- is one past the standing count because undone moves are always the latest.
-- The guards restate the rules' own, read from the puzzle, so that a move
-- the rules would refuse is never recorded: a chained stimulus the rules
-- refused would leave the record standing.

rule Place {
    when: Place(target, digit)

    requires: target.board.puzzle.status = unsolved
    requires: not target.puzzle_cell.is_given
    requires: digit >= 1 and digit <= config.side

    -- The board's own guard, stricter than the rules': PlaceDigit lets a
    -- cell holding the player's digit take the same one again, but a move
    -- that changes nothing is not a move, so it is refused here and never
    -- joins the record or discards what was undone.
    requires: target.puzzle_cell.digit = null or target.puzzle_cell.digit != digit

    -- Read before anything changes: the peers whose note holds the digit are
    -- the ones upkeep strikes it from, hidden notes included, and no others.
    let board = target.board
    let index = board.standing_moves.count + 1
    let struck = board.cells where puzzle_cell in target.puzzle_cell.peers and digit in note

    -- The target's own note is kept, unshown, beneath the digit. The digit
    -- goes into the cell by the rules' PlaceDigit and by nothing here.
    ensures:
        for undone in board.undone_moves:
            not exists undone
        Move.created(
            board: board,
            index: index,
            kind: place,
            target: target,
            digit: digit,
            digit_before: target.puzzle_cell.digit,
            struck_from: struck,
            is_undone: false
        )
        for peer in struck:
            peer.note = peer.note - { digit }
        sudoku/PlaceDigit(cell: target.puzzle_cell, digit: digit)
}

rule Erase {
    when: Erase(target)

    requires: target.board.puzzle.status = unsolved
    requires: target.holds_players_digit

    let board = target.board
    let index = board.standing_moves.count + 1

    -- The note the digit stood on is shown again once the digit is gone. The
    -- peers' notes get nothing back: an erasure is not the reverse of a
    -- placement, and only undo is. The digit leaves the cell by the rules'
    -- EraseDigit and by nothing here.
    ensures:
        for undone in board.undone_moves:
            not exists undone
        Move.created(
            board: board,
            index: index,
            kind: erase,
            target: target,
            digit: null,
            digit_before: target.puzzle_cell.digit,
            struck_from: {},
            is_undone: false
        )
        sudoku/EraseDigit(cell: target.puzzle_cell)
}

rule WriteMark {
    when: WriteMark(target, digit)

    requires: target.board.puzzle.status = unsolved
    requires: target.accepts_marks
    requires: digit >= 1 and digit <= config.side
    requires: digit not in target.note

    let board = target.board
    let index = board.standing_moves.count + 1

    -- Nothing asks the mark to be true: as on paper, a player may write a
    -- digit a placed peer rules out, and the mark is theirs to strike.
    ensures:
        for undone in board.undone_moves:
            not exists undone
        Move.created(
            board: board,
            index: index,
            kind: write_mark,
            target: target,
            digit: digit,
            digit_before: null,
            struck_from: {},
            is_undone: false
        )
        target.note = target.note + { digit }
}

rule StrikeMark {
    when: StrikeMark(target, digit)

    requires: target.board.puzzle.status = unsolved
    requires: target.accepts_marks
    requires: digit in target.note

    let board = target.board
    let index = board.standing_moves.count + 1

    ensures:
        for undone in board.undone_moves:
            not exists undone
        Move.created(
            board: board,
            index: index,
            kind: strike_mark,
            target: target,
            digit: digit,
            digit_before: null,
            struck_from: {},
            is_undone: false
        )
        target.note = target.note - { digit }
}

-- Taking back and re-taking --------------------------------

rule Undo {
    when: Undo(board)

    requires: board.can_undo

    -- The one move to take back, read before it is marked undone.
    let taken_back = board.latest_standing_moves

    -- By kind, the exact reverse of what the move did. A placement leaves
    -- the cell as it stood, digit or none, and gives the digit back to every
    -- note upkeep struck it from; an erasure puts the digit back; a written
    -- mark is struck and a struck mark written. The cell's digit is written
    -- back to what a move the rules admitted left there, so no rule of the
    -- rules is asked to run again.
    ensures:
        for move in taken_back:
            move.is_undone = true
            if move.kind = place:
                move.target.puzzle_cell.digit = move.digit_before
                for peer in move.struck_from:
                    peer.note = peer.note + { move.digit }
            if move.kind = erase:
                move.target.puzzle_cell.digit = move.digit_before
            if move.kind = write_mark:
                move.target.note = move.target.note - { move.digit }
            if move.kind = strike_mark:
                move.target.note = move.target.note + { move.digit }
}

rule Redo {
    when: Redo(board)

    requires: board.can_redo

    let retaken = board.next_to_redo

    -- The mirror of Undo: what the move did, done again. Nothing has moved
    -- since it was taken back, because a new move would have discarded it,
    -- so what it did then is exactly what it does now.
    ensures:
        for move in retaken:
            move.is_undone = false
            if move.kind = place:
                move.target.puzzle_cell.digit = move.digit
                for peer in move.struck_from:
                    peer.note = peer.note - { move.digit }
            if move.kind = erase:
                move.target.puzzle_cell.digit = null
            if move.kind = write_mark:
                move.target.note = move.target.note + { move.digit }
            if move.kind = strike_mark:
                move.target.note = move.target.note - { move.digit }
}

-- The check -------------------------------------------------

rule CheckCell {
    when: CheckCell(target)

    requires: target.board.puzzle.status = unsolved
    requires: target.holds_players_digit

    let board = target.board
    -- Read before the check is kept, as a move's index is: one past the
    -- checks already on the record.
    let index = board.checks.count + 1

    -- solution_digit_at is a black box: the digit the one solution of these
    -- givens holds at this row and column. The givens are well-posed, since
    -- SetPuzzle admits no others, so there is one solution and one digit.
    -- solver.allium says how it is found. The answer is a yes or a no and
    -- the digit itself goes nowhere.
    ensures:
        Check.created(
            board: board,
            index: index,
            after_move: board.standing_moves.count,
            target: target,
            digit: target.puzzle_cell.digit,
            is_right: target.puzzle_cell.digit = solution_digit_at(board.puzzle.givens, target.row, target.column)
        )
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A state is what stands once a stimulus and the rules it sets off have run,
-- as in sudoku.allium. OpenBoard and LayOutBoard follow SetPuzzle and
-- LayOutGrid in one such step, so a board with no cells is not a state
-- anything sees. Three properties compare one state with the next and so are
-- prose, on the Playing surface: that undo and redo are exact, that a note
-- waits beneath a digit, and that a check is never taken back.

invariant OneBoardToAPuzzle {
    for a in Boards:
        for b in Boards:
            a != b implies a.puzzle != b.puzzle
}

invariant TheBoardIsWhole {
    for board in Boards:
        board.cells.count = config.side * config.side
}

invariant OneBoardCellToAPosition {
    for a in BoardCells:
        for b in BoardCells:
            a != b and a.board = b.board
                implies a.row != b.row or a.column != b.column
}

invariant MarksAreDigits {
    for cell in BoardCells:
        cell.note.all(d => d >= 1 and d <= config.side)
}

invariant NoMarkInAGiven {
    for cell in BoardCells:
        cell.puzzle_cell.is_given implies cell.note.count = 0
}

invariant MovesNeverTouchAGiven {
    for move in Moves:
        not move.target.puzzle_cell.is_given
}

invariant MoveIndicesRunFromOne {
    for move in Moves:
        move.index >= 1 and move.index <= move.board.moves.count
}

invariant MoveIndicesAreDistinct {
    for a in Moves:
        for b in Moves:
            a != b and a.board = b.board implies a.index != b.index
}

invariant MovesCarryWhatTheirKindNeeds {
    for move in Moves:
        (move.kind = place implies move.digit != null)
            and (move.kind = erase
                implies move.digit = null and move.digit_before != null)
            and ((move.kind = write_mark or move.kind = strike_mark)
                implies move.digit != null
                    and move.digit_before = null
                    and move.struck_from.count = 0)
}

-- Every move after an undone one is undone: what was taken back is the tail
-- of the record, and a new move discards the tail.
invariant UndoneMovesAreTheLatest {
    for move in Moves:
        move.is_undone
            implies move.board.moves.all(o => o.index < move.index or o.is_undone)
}

-- What redo re-takes is the move that comes right after the standing ones:
-- the earliest undone move, whose index is one past the standing count.
invariant WhatIsReTakenComesNext {
    for move in Moves:
        move.is_next_to_redo
            implies move.index = move.board.standing_moves.count + 1
}

invariant ASolvedBoardHasNothingUndone {
    for board in Boards:
        board.puzzle.status = solved implies board.undone_moves.count = 0
}

-- Upkeep reaches peers and no others. That every peer's note lost the digit
-- when it was placed is the guarantee's to say, since a mark written later
-- may hold it again.
invariant UpkeepStrikesPeersOfAPlacement {
    for move in Moves:
        move.struck_from.all(p => p.puzzle_cell in move.target.puzzle_cell.peers)
}

-- The reading at the latest standing move is the board. Absent digits are
-- compared as absences, since a comparison with nothing is never true.
invariant TheMovesReplayToTheBoard {
    for move in Moves:
        move.is_latest_standing
            implies move.board.cells.all(c =>
                ((move.digit_after(c) = null and c.puzzle_cell.digit = null)
                    or move.digit_after(c) = c.puzzle_cell.digit)
                and move.note_after(c) = c.note)
}

invariant ChecksRunFromOne {
    for check in Checks:
        check.index >= 1 and check.index <= check.board.checks.count
}

invariant CheckIndicesAreDistinct {
    for a in Checks:
        for b in Checks:
            a != b and a.board = b.board implies a.index != b.index
}

invariant ACheckIsOnAPlayersCell {
    for check in Checks:
        not check.target.puzzle_cell.is_given
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

-- What writing a board down and having it again must satisfy. It names no
-- form and no place: a record is whatever a store is handed, and the store
-- is a port's.
contract Recording {
    write: (board: Board) -> Record

    -- Reopening puts the record's givens to sudoku/SetPuzzle, so a record of
    -- a puzzle the rules would not pose is refused, and the board OpenBoard
    -- opens on the puzzle so set is made whole from the record: every digit,
    -- every note shown or waiting, every move with its index and whether it
    -- is undone, and every check as written. No move is re-made and no rule
    -- of this module runs for what the record holds. The puzzle is a new one
    -- with the one board OpenBoard gives it, so OneBoardToAPuzzle stands.
    reopen: (record: Record) -> Board

    @invariant ReopeningIsExact
        -- A board reopened from the record of a board is that board: the
        -- same puzzle with the same givens and the same digits, the same
        -- note in every cell whether shown or waiting beneath a digit, the
        -- same moves in the same order with the same ones undone, and the
        -- same checks. Every reading, undo, redo and check answers on the
        -- reopened board as it would have on the written one.

    @invariant WritingChangesNothing
        -- Writing a board down leaves it exactly as it was, and can be done
        -- at any point, solved or not.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

-- What a player has in front of them, and what they may do. It draws
-- nothing: every state named here — given, conflicting, undone, right — is
-- owed a non-colour indication and an accessible name by whatever draws it,
-- and the words are the game's.
surface Playing {
    context board: Board

    exposes:
        board.puzzle.status
        board.puzzle.is_full
        board.puzzle.is_consistent
        board.can_undo
        board.can_redo
        for cell in board.cells:
            cell.row
            cell.column
            cell.puzzle_cell.digit
            cell.puzzle_cell.is_given
            cell.puzzle_cell.is_conflicting
            cell.note when cell.shows_note
        for move in board.moves:
            move.index
            move.kind
            move.target
            move.digit
            move.is_undone
            for cell in board.cells:
                move.digit_after(cell)
                move.note_after(cell)
        for check in board.checks:
            check.index
            check.after_move
            check.target
            check.digit
            check.is_right

    provides:
        for cell in board.cells:
            Place(cell, digit)
                when board.puzzle.status = unsolved and not cell.puzzle_cell.is_given
            Erase(cell)
                when board.puzzle.status = unsolved and cell.holds_players_digit
            WriteMark(cell, digit)
                when board.puzzle.status = unsolved and cell.accepts_marks
            StrikeMark(cell, digit)
                when board.puzzle.status = unsolved and cell.accepts_marks and cell.note.count > 0
            CheckCell(cell)
                when board.puzzle.status = unsolved and cell.holds_players_digit
        Undo(board) when board.can_undo
        Redo(board) when board.can_redo

    @guarantee EveryMoveIsTheBoards
        -- In play a digit is placed or erased through this surface and no
        -- other, so every placement and erasure is recorded, kept up and
        -- reversible. What the surface puts to the rules is the rules' own
        -- move, and a move the rules would refuse is refused here first;
        -- a placement the board refuses, the standing digit placed again,
        -- never reaches them.

    @guarantee UndoAndRedoAreExact
        -- After undo the board stands exactly as it did before the move
        -- taken back: every digit, every note shown or waiting, every mark
        -- upkeep struck given back to the notes it was struck from and no
        -- others. After redo it stands exactly as it did after that move.

    @guarantee ANewMoveDiscardsWhatWasUndone
        -- A move made while moves are undone discards every one of them:
        -- they are gone from the record and cannot be re-taken.

    @guarantee AMarkIsAMove
        -- Writing a mark and striking one are recorded, taken back and
        -- re-taken exactly as a placement is.

    @guarantee UndoStopsWithThePuzzle
        -- Once the puzzle is solved nothing is taken back or re-taken. What
        -- is offered follows can_undo and can_redo and nothing else.

    @guarantee EveryPastBoardIsReadable
        -- For every move on the record, standing or undone, and whether or
        -- not the puzzle is solved, the board as it stood once that move and
        -- no later one had been made can be read, and reading it takes
        -- nothing back.

    @guarantee GivensNeverMove
        -- No move targets a given cell, no note is written in one, and undo
        -- and redo never write to one.

    @guarantee ANoteWaitsBeneathADigit
        -- A cell's note is shown while the cell is empty. A placed digit
        -- hides it and an erasure shows it again as it was, less whatever
        -- upkeep struck from it meanwhile. Nothing but upkeep, undo and redo
        -- changes a note beneath a digit.

    @guarantee EveryCheckIsKept
        -- Every check stays on the record for as long as the board does, in
        -- the order asked, and is never taken back. Its after_move is how
        -- many moves stood when it was asked, not a reference: after an undo
        -- and a new move, that count may name a different move.

    @guarantee ACheckNeverTellsTheDigit
        -- The answer to a check is yes or no about the digit standing in
        -- the cell. No check reveals a digit, and a check is offered only
        -- on a cell holding a digit of the player's own.

    @guarantee TheSolutionIsNeverShown
        -- Nothing this surface exposes is read from the solution except the
        -- yes or no of a check the player asked for.
}

-- Where a board is written down and had again. No board is its context:
-- reopening is how a board comes to be from a record, so this surface
-- stands whether or not one is open, and write takes the board it writes.
surface Reopening {
    contracts:
        fulfils Recording

    @guarantee AReopenedBoardIsTheSameBoard
        -- Reopening the record of a board gives a board Playing cannot tell
        -- from the one written: the same is exposed, the same is offered,
        -- and every move, reading and check answers the same.

    @guarantee ARecordIsEnoughOnItsOwn
        -- A record carries everything reopening needs. Nothing else is
        -- consulted: not the store it came from, not the solution's digits,
        -- since SetPuzzle's guard asks the solver for its verdict and nothing
        -- more, not any other record and not a board already open, since
        -- there need be none.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Every check is kept, which invites a count: of checks made, of answers
-- that were no. Nobody has decided whether anything is counted, or shown.
open question "What, if anything, is counted from the checks - wrong answers, checks made - and is it shown to the player?"
